00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.160
Hi, what is tokenization?&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.120
You know, that line of code you use from&nbsp;
Huggingface Transformers and never care about.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.600
In today’s video, we will see how this works!

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.760
Tokenization is that part in&nbsp;
pre-processing that is done to text&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.560
before throwing it into your&nbsp;
favorite language model, say BERT.&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.640
It’s a pre-processing step, so it is&nbsp;
independent of the later training process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.520
but it's crucial for most transformers.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.520
Without tokenization, each word of the&nbsp;
English vocabulary would have to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.880
assigned a unique vector on the input side
and, when predicting the masked word in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.520
the output, the choice would have&nbsp;
to consider the whole vocabulary.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.560
And if you believe that the English vocabulary&nbsp;
that one can find in a dictionary, is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:13.920
huge, think about how it can grow even faster&nbsp;
with all words that are not in a dictionary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:22.000
like compound words created on the fly by someone&nbsp;
as a stylistic figure. Or all possible typos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.760
right? A mistyped word is also a word and should&nbsp;
have a way to be processed by a language model.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:36.080
This is where tokenization comes in, to&nbsp;
at least partially, solve this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.320
by creating a smaller vocabulary of&nbsp;
word building blocks. This hopes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.000
cover as much of the dictionary as&nbsp;
possible, including unexpected words.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.520
Therefore, tokenizers first segment the sentence&nbsp;
into words (harder for some languages than for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.840
others, looking at you, Chinese, Thai and Korean,&nbsp;
because you do not have whitespace separation).&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.640
Then the words are parsed into sub-words, so&nbsp;
that even though supposedly the dictionary does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:15.120
not have the word “pre-processing” in it, and&nbsp;
therefore no vector representation to associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.040
with it, the tokenizer can divide&nbsp;
the word into “pre” and “processing”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.040
supposing that “pre” and “processing” are already&nbsp;
in the vocabulary and have a vector association.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:33.840
And this works great in very regular kinds of&nbsp;
words that can be decomposed into known blocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:41.440
but does not solve the problem of noisy text, full&nbsp;
of typos where the possibilities of mistyping are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:48.480
so many, that a fall-back to a known subword is&nbsp;
either impossible or leads to misunderstandings.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.240
So tokenization, being so rigid can become&nbsp;
a problem, an even bigger one when working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.400
with multiple languages where the&nbsp;
out-of-vocabulary problem becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.800
even greater with every additional&nbsp;
language the model has to handle.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:09.680
Even more, the downside of tokenization is that&nbsp;
on downstream applications, you are kind-of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.600
forced to use the same tokenizer as the model&nbsp;
was trained with, because the trained model is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.880
very much dependent on the tokenization&nbsp;
it is used to. Even though your task might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.160
benefit from other tokenizations, you&nbsp;
now must stick with the initial ones.&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.080
No, you're not allowed to see&nbsp;
other tokenizers, Ms. Coffee Bean!

